WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.001 --> 00:00:07.390
"Can Getting Transfusions of
Young Blood Slow Aging?"

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.240
Vampires 2.0?

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.020
The ethical quandaries
of young blood infusion

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.180
in the quest for eternal life.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.200
In an episode of the TV series
Silicon Valley entitled,

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.160
"The Blood Boy,"

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.860
a tech guru hires
a young assistant

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.920
from whom to transfuse
pints of blood

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.320
in an attempt to slow down
his aging.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.000
The restoration of youth

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.760
by bathing in or drinking
the blood of youth

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.740
is a centuries-old trope

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.140
dating back at least
to the futile attempt

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.900
of ailing Pope Innocent VIII,

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.800
who, in the 1490s,
failed to live up to his name

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.520
by apparently drinking the blood
of three ten-year-old boys,

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.380
who, though a bargain--

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.420
"costing only
one ducat apiece"--

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.160
died in the process.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.780
Might there be something
to it, though?

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:01.080
Though young blood did
not have a significant impact

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.060
on lifespan, otherwise,

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:05.640
infusing the blood
of young animals into old

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.880
did have a rejuvenating effects
on multiple organs

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.160
similar to
full-blown parabiosis,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:12.720
as I talked about
in the last video.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:16.260
In fact, not only does the
infusion of young mouse blood

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.380
improve age-related cognitive
dysfunction in old mice,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.220
young human blood,
taken from umbilical cords,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.060
worked too,

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.060
in mice engineered to not reject
the foreign tissue.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.880
This led
to ongoing clinical trials

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.400
offering weekly infusions
of blood products

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.840
from young donors
to patients

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.300
with mild to moderate
Alzheimer's disease.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.960
But there may be
an easier way.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.840
Yes, the injection of blood
from young mice into old

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.580
can improve memory
and learning,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.820
suggesting there's some kind
of restorative youth factor,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:51.120
but the injection of blood
from old mice into young

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.040
can worsen memory and learning,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.420
suggesting that instead

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.300
there's some sort
of debilitating aging factor.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:00.660
Or maybe the old blood
is just diluting

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.580
the revitalizing factor
in the young mouse,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.500
or, for that matter,
maybe the young blood

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.900
is diluting
the debilitating factor

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:07.440
in the old mouse.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:10.620
The fact that old blood
appears to make things worse

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.720
more than young blood
makes them better

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:14.760
suggests maybe the latter
is the case,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:19.500
but you don't know,
until you put it to the test.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.360
Ready for a mind-blowing shift
in perspective?

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.780
Instead of infusing young blood
into old mice,

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.280
researchers at UC Berkeley

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.100
just diluted
the blood of old mice

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:35.220
by siphoning off the plasma,
the liquid portion of the blood,

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.500
and essentially replacing
half of it with water.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.580
If the regeneration seen
in heterochronic parabiosis

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:42.720
and blood transfusions

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.660
were due to some
fountain of youth factor

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.320
in young blood,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.120
then nothing should happen,
right?

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.580
But if all the young blood
was doing

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.340
was diluting
some debilitating aging factor,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:55.200
then the water should work
just as well,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:56.340
and it did.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.040
The rejuvenation in the liver

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.140
was similar to parabiosis
or transfusion,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.800
and in the muscles and the brain
it was even better.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:07.740
So, most if not all the benefits

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.740
could be replicated
by simple dilution,

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:14.400
including an improvement
in cognitive capacity.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.080
That's good news,

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.900
since there's already
an FDA approved procedure

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.200
in use today known
as therapeutic plasma exchange.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:26.700
It's usually used to filter out
toxins or autoimmune antibodies,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.140
but why not try using it
to try to dilute old blood

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.700
to treat Alzheimer's disease?

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:36.480
And that's exactly what
researchers did.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:38.520
Hundreds of Alzheimer's patients

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.460
were randomized to a therapeutic
plasma exchange procedure,

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.160
or a placebo (sham) procedure,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.560
and though it didn't seem
to help those

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.660
with mild Alzheimer's disease,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:52.980
those with moderate Alzheimer's
randomized to the real procedure

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.880
experienced about 60% less
cognitive and functional decline

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:58.920
over a period of 14 months.

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.700
This is in stark contrast
to mainstay treatments

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.800
for Alzheimer's
like the drug memantine

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.120
that can help with symptoms

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.520
but don't actually change
or alter

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.960
the course of the disease.

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:13.200
And the added advantage
over blood transfusions,

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:16.380
as a Director of an Institute
of Biomedical Ethics put it:

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.720
"There is something peculiar

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.660
about the old literally
feeding on the young."

